  "code": "1203",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 99.9971%; height: 133.889px;\" border=\"1\"><colgroup><col style=\"width: 49.8607%;\"><col style=\"width: 49.8607%;\"></colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.4722px;\">\n<td>Result Interpretion&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Reference range in mmol/l</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.4722px;\">\n<td>Normal&nbsp;</td>\n<td>0.40-1.20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.4722px;\">\n<td>Warning</td>\n<td>1.20-1.50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.4722px;\">\n<td>Risk of intoxication&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&gt;1.50</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Comments</strong>:</p>\n<p>Lithium is used for treatment of bipolar disorders and is also considered as drug of choice for prevention of chronic cluster headache. Complete absorption of lithium occurs after 6-8 hrs of oral administration. Half-life of the drug in serum/ plasma after 17-36 hours and onset of action is slow (5-10 days). It has a varied tissue distribution and symptoms of acute intoxication at times may not correlate with serum levels. Toxicity can be acute and chronic as well. Renal toxicity and hypothyroidism are known to be associated with lithium drugs. It is advisable to periodically monitor creatinine and TSH levels as well in patients taking lithium.</p>\n<p>It is recommended to use a test for lithium 12 hours after taking drug to monitor adequate therapy.</p>",
